.TH ZERO 1
.SH NAME
zero \- a chess engine that searches as AlphaZero does, with lc0's networks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zero
[
.B -b
.I batch
] [
.B -g
.I backend
] [
.B -n
.I network
]
.br
.B zero
[
.I option ...
]
.B -e
.I position
.br
.B zero
[
.I option ...
]
.B -p
.I position
.br
.B zero
[
.I option ...
]
.B -B
[
.I batch
]
.br
.B zero
.B -t
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Zero
is a chess engine speaking UCI, the Universal Chess Interface, on its standard input and output,
as
.IR chess (1)
runs one.
It evaluates positions with a neural network of Leela Chess Zero's (lc0's),
read from lc0's own weights file, and searches as AlphaZero does:
a Monte Carlo tree search that chooses each move to look at by PUCT,
the network's prior for it against what the search has found,
and takes the network's value of each new position in place of a playout.
It gathers the positions to evaluate in batches, as lc0 does,
the visits in flight on each line counting against its share of exploration
so that the next line taken is another where it can be,
and evaluates a batch at once.
Its moves are
.IR chess 's.
.PP
The networks are in
.BR /lib/nn :
.BI maia N .pb.gz\fR,
Maia's, for
.I N
from 1100 to 1900 by hundreds,
each trained to play the move that a human player of that rating plays;
and
.BR 703810.pb.gz ,
Leela's, the last network of its T70 run,
ten residual blocks of 128 filters.
.I Zero
runs a network of lc0's classical input (112 planes of 8 by 8: the last eight positions,
castling, the side to move and the 50-move count),
a residual tower with or without squeeze and excitation,
a convolutional policy head of 1858 moves,
and a value head of win, draw and loss, or of one value.
It does not run the attention networks of lc0's later runs,
and refuses a network whose layers do not fit one another, saying which part is wrong.
.PP
It runs the network on the GPU, by
.IR gpu (3),
where there is one, and on the CPU otherwise.
Its options:
.TP
.B -b
The most positions it evaluates at once, 32 if not told.
.TP
.B -g
The backend:
.BR auto ,
the GPU if it can, then the CPU;
.BR gpu ,
or
.BR cpu .
.TP
.B -n
The network, as the
.B Network
option.
.I Zero
assumes none: with neither
.B -n
nor
.BR Network ,
it searches nothing and says so,
and
.B -e
and
.B -B
refuse to start.
.PP
With
.BR -e ,
.I zero
evaluates the
.I position
(as UCI's
.B position
command takes it:
.B startpos
or
.BI fen " fen"\fR,
then
.B moves
and the moves)
once and prints the backend, the win, draw and loss and the moves-left estimate,
and each legal move with the policy's logit for it;
with
.BR -p ,
it prints the position's input planes,
each as its number, its squares as 64 bits, and the value of a square that is set;
with
.BR -B ,
it evaluates
.I batch
copies of the start position for three seconds and says how many positions it evaluated a second;
with
.BR -t ,
it prints its table of lc0's 1858 policy moves, one a line,
then the 4672 entries of the convolutional policy's map from planes to moves.
.SS UCI
.I Zero
takes
.BR uci ,
.BR isready ,
.BR setoption ,
.BR ucinewgame ,
.BR position ,
.BR go ,
.B stop
and
.BR quit .
A
.B position
with a FEN it cannot read is refused, with an
.B "info string"
saying so, and the position it had is kept.
While it searches, it answers
.B isready
and takes
.BR stop ;
the other commands wait for the search to end, and
.B quit
ends a
.B "go infinite"
at once.
.B Go
takes
.BR infinite ,
.BI nodes " n"\fR,
.BI movetime " ms"\fR,
and
.BR wtime ,
.BR btime ,
.BR winc ,
.B binc
and
.BR movestogo ,
of which it spends the side to move's time over 25 moves, or the moves to go, and half its increment.
A search ends at the first of its nodes and its time;
an infinite one ends at
.BR stop .
While it searches it says, every half second and at its end,
.EX
	info depth 5 seldepth 12 time 1084 nodes 432 nps 398 score cp -9 pv c7c5 g1f3 ...
.EE
where the depth is the mean depth of its visits, the seldepth the deepest,
and the nodes the visits to the root;
the score, from the side to move, is lc0's centipawns,
90 tan(1.5637541897 q) for the expected outcome
.I q
(1 a win, \-1 a loss) of the move,
or a mate in one when the move mates.
Its options:
.TP
.B Nodes
The visits a search makes unless
.B go
says otherwise; 0 if not set, for no limit but time,
or, with no time either, until the tree holds some four million moves; 1 is the policy's own move,
as Maia is meant to play.
.TP
.B Network
The network's file, as
.B -n
names it to start.
.TP
.B Temperature
0, the move with the most visits (with one, the policy's best move);
above 0, a move drawn at random, each as likely as its visits, or its policy, to the power of 1/temperature.
.TP
.B MultiPV
How many moves its
.B info
lines show, the most visited first, each with
.BR multipv .
.TP
.B VerboseMoveStats
After a search, a line for each move, as lc0 writes it:
.EX
	info string e2e4  (322 ) N:     281 (+ 0) (P: 50.24%) (Q:  0.04612) (V:  0.0383)
.EE
its policy index, its visits, the visits in flight, its prior, its value from the search
and the network's value of the position it leads to, from the side that moves;
then the root's line,
.BR node .
.TP
.B PolicyTemperature
The temperature of the policy's softmax over the legal moves, 1.359 as in lc0.
.TP
.B Backend
As
.BR -g .
.PP
The search's other constants are lc0's: its PUCT constant
1.745 + 3.894 ln((N+38739)/38739) at a node of
.I N
visits, times the root of its children's visits,
and a first-play value of the parent's value less 0.33 times the root of the policy already visited.
A position that has come three times, or twice within the search, is a draw,
as are those of the 50-move rule and those with no mating material.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR chess (1)
names engines in
.BR /lib/chess/engines ,
a line each: Maia at 1100, playing its policy's move, and Leela searching:
.IP
.EX
engine=maia1100 cmd=zero Network=/lib/nn/maia1100.pb.gz Nodes=1
engine=leela cmd=zero Network=/lib/nn/703810.pb.gz Nodes=0
.EE
.PP
so that
.B "Chess -e maia1100 game.pgn"
plays it.
Evaluate a game's moves with Leela's network, 800 visits each:
.IP
.EX
chess -a -e '{ echo setoption name Nodes value 800; cat } | zero -n /lib/nn/703810.pb.gz' game.pgn
.EE
.PP
Show Maia's policy for the position after 1. e4:
.IP
.EX
{ echo setoption name VerboseMoveStats value true; echo setoption name Nodes value 1
  echo position startpos moves e2e4; echo go; echo quit } | zero -n /lib/nn/maia1500.pb.gz
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/nn/703810.pb.gz
.TP
.BI /lib/nn/maia N .pb.gz
Maia's networks
.TP
.B /lib/nn/703810.pb.gz
Leela's network 703810
.TP
.B /dev/gpu
the GPU,
.IR gpu (3)
